[{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#Lagrangian-Neural-Network","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"A Lagrangian neural network learns a scalar Lagrangian L(q dotq) instead of the dynamics themselves. The dynamics are then whatever that Lagrangian implies, through the Euler–Lagrange equations\n\n    fracddtfracpartialLpartialdotq = fracpartialLpartialq\n\nThis is the Lagrangian counterpart of the Hamiltonian neural network of HamiltonianNeuralNetworks.jl: there the network is a Hamiltonian and the dynamics come from a Poisson tensor, here the network is a Lagrangian and the dynamics come from a variational principle.","title":"Lagrangian Neural Network"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#The-loss","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"Expanding the Euler–Lagrange equations along a trajectory gives\n\n    nabla_dotqnabla_dotqLddotq\n        + (nabla_qnabla_dotqL)^Tdotq\n        = nabla_qL\n\nso the acceleration the learned Lagrangian predicts is\n\n    ddotq = (nabla_dotqnabla_dotqL)^-1\n               left( nabla_qL - (nabla_qnabla_dotqL)^Tdotq right)\n\nwhich is what LagrangianNeuralNetworks.lagrangian_acceleration computes and what LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LNNLoss compares against the data.\n\nThe transpose is not cosmetic. nabla_qnabla_dotqL is indexed i j = partial^2Lpartialq_ipartialdotq_j, while the chain rule contracts the first index with dotq. It disappears only for a Lagrangian whose position–velocity coupling happens to be symmetric, which is why the test for this asserts a closed form whose coupling deliberately is not.","title":"The loss"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#Why-the-derivatives-are-symbolic","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"The loss needs the second derivative of the network with respect to its input, and then its own derivative with respect to the network parameters. Taking the inner one with Zygote inside the loss does not work: the parameter gradient then fails with MethodError: no method matching getindex(::IdDict{Any, Any}). The gradient and Hessian are therefore built as symbolic expressions with SymbolicNeuralNetworks and compiled, so that only one differentiation is left for Zygote to do. This is the same route that hamiltonian_vector_field of HamiltonianNeuralNetworks.jl takes, and for the same reason.","title":"Why the derivatives are symbolic"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#Training-on-positions-alone","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LNNLoss needs ddotq in the data. When only positions are available, LagrangianNeuralNetworks.VariationalMidpointLoss trains the same architecture through the discrete Euler–Lagrange equations of the midpoint discrete Lagrangian\n\n    L_d(q_n q_n+1) = Deltat\n        Lleft( fracq_n + q_n+12 fracq_n+1 - q_nDeltat right)\n\nwhich ask that D_2L_d(q_n q_n+1) + D_1L_d(q_n+1 q_n+2) = 0 along the data. Only the first derivative of the network is needed for this, because the chain rule through the midpoint is written out rather than taken with a nested Zygote.gradient.","title":"Training on positions alone"},{"category":"section","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#Library-Functions","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"","title":"Library Functions"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LNNLoss","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"LNNLoss <: NetworkLoss\n\nThe loss for a Lagrangian neural network.\n\nThe network output is a scalar Lagrangian L(q dotq). The loss compares the acceleration its Euler–Lagrange equations predict against the acceleration in the data:\n\n    mathttloss(mathcalNN mathttinput mathttoutput)\n        = ddotq(mathttinput) - mathttoutput  mathttoutput\n\nwhere mathttinput stacks q on dotq and is therefore 2n rows tall, and mathttoutput is ddotq and is n rows tall. See lagrangian_acceleration for ddotq.\n\nConstructor\n\nThis can be called with a LagrangianNeuralNetwork as its only argument:\n\nLNNLoss(arch)\n\nFunctor\n\nloss(model, ps, input, output)\nloss(ps, input, output) # equivalent to the above\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.LNNLoss"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.lagrangian_acceleration","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"lagrangian_acceleration(arch::LagrangianNeuralNetwork)\n\nCompute an executable expression of the acceleration that the Lagrangian arch predicts.\n\nThe Euler–Lagrange equations of a Lagrangian L(q dotq) are\n\n    fracddtfracpartialLpartialdotq = fracpartialLpartialq\n\nwhich, expanded along a trajectory, reads\n\n    nabla_dotqnabla_dotqLddotq\n        + (nabla_qnabla_dotqL)^Tdotq\n        = nabla_qL\n\nso the acceleration is\n\n    ddotq = (nabla_dotqnabla_dotqL)^-1\n               left( nabla_qL - (nabla_qnabla_dotqL)^Tdotq right)\n\nThe transpose is not cosmetic. nabla_qnabla_dotqL is indexed i j = partial^2Lpartialq_ipartialdotq_j, while the chain rule contracts the first index with dotq. It disappears only for a Lagrangian whose position–velocity coupling happens to be symmetric.\n\nImplementation\n\nThe gradient and the Hessian of the network with respect to its input are built as symbolic expressions with SymbolicNeuralNetworks.Jacobian — applied once, and then again to its own result — and compiled with build_nn_function. They are not taken with Zygote inside the loss: differentiating a loss that itself contains a Zygote.gradient with respect to the network parameters fails with MethodError: no method matching getindex(::IdDict{Any, Any}). This is the route hamiltonian_vector_field of HamiltonianNeuralNetworks.jl takes, and for the same reason.\n\nThe compiled functions flatten a batch along the columns — (1 2ncdotB) for the gradient and (2n 2ncdotB) for the Hessian — so both are reshaped before use. Trailing input dimensions beyond the first are flattened on the way in and restored on the way out, so an input of size (2n s B), which is what Batch hands the loss, yields an acceleration of size (n s B).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.lagrangian_acceleration"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks._euler_lagrange_acceleration","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"_euler_lagrange_acceleration(H, g, q̇)\n\nSolve the Euler–Lagrange equations of one sample for the acceleration.\n\nH is the full 2ntimes2n Hessian of the Lagrangian with respect to its input, g the full 2n gradient, and q̇ the n velocity components of that same input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks._euler_lagrange_acceleration"},{"category":"type","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.VariationalMidpointLoss","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"VariationalMidpointLoss <: NetworkLoss\n\nThe loss that trains a Lagrangian neural network on positions alone, through the discrete Euler–Lagrange equations of the midpoint discrete Lagrangian.\n\nIt needs three consecutive positions and the timestep between them — no velocities and no accelerations.\n\nThe midpoint discrete Lagrangian is\n\n    L_d(q_n q_n+1) = Deltat\n        Lleft( fracq_n + q_n+12 fracq_n+1 - q_nDeltat right)\n\nand the discrete Euler–Lagrange equations ask that\n\n    D_2L_d(q_n q_n+1) + D_1L_d(q_n+1 q_n+2) = 0 \n\nThe loss is the norm of that residual, relative to the norm of the two derivatives it is built from, so a Lagrangian scaled by a constant gives the same number.\n\nImplementation\n\nThe two slot derivatives are written out with the chain rule rather than taken with a nested Zygote.gradient:\n\n    D_1L_d = fracDeltat2nabla_qL - nabla_dotqL qquad\n    D_2L_d = fracDeltat2nabla_qL + nabla_dotqL\n\nboth evaluated at the midpoint. Only the first derivative of the network with respect to its input is then needed, and that comes from a compiled symbolic expression exactly as that of hamiltonian_vector_field of HamiltonianNeuralNetworks.jl does. Written the other way — a Zygote.gradient inside the loss — the parameter gradient fails with MethodError: no method matching getindex(::IdDict{Any, Any}).\n\nConstructor\n\nVariationalMidpointLoss(arch, timestep)\n\nwhere arch is a LagrangianNeuralNetwork.\n\nFunctor\n\nloss(model, ps, input, output)\nloss(ps, input, output) # equivalent to the above\n\ninput stacks q_n on q_n+1 and so has 2n rows; output is q_n+2 and has n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.VariationalMidpointLoss"},{"category":"function","location":"architectures/lagrangian_neural_network/#LagrangianNeuralNetworks._discrete_lagrangian_derivatives","page":"Lagrangian Neural Network","text":"_discrete_lagrangian_derivatives(loss, ps, qa, qb)\n\nReturn (D₁Lᵈ, D₂Lᵈ) of the midpoint discrete Lagrangian on the pair (qa, qb), one column per sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks._discrete_lagrangian_derivatives"},{"category":"section","location":"#LagrangianNeuralNetworks.jl","page":"Home","text":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks learns a scalar Lagrangian L(q dotq) with a neural network, and takes the dynamics from that Lagrangian through the Euler–Lagrange equations.\n\nIt exports the architecture LagrangianNeuralNetwork and two losses: LNNLoss, which compares the acceleration that the learned Lagrangian predicts against accelerations in the data, and VariationalMidpointLoss, which needs positions only. The training itself — the data loader, the batches, the optimiser and train! — is GeometricMachineLearning's.\n\nusing LagrangianNeuralNetworks\nusing GeometricMachineLearning: NeuralNetwork\n\narch = LagrangianNeuralNetwork(2; width = 4, nhidden = 1)\nnn = NeuralNetwork(arch)\nloss = LNNLoss(arch)\n\nThe page Lagrangian Neural Network derives the loss and documents the functions.","title":"LagrangianNeuralNetworks.jl"}]
